memfd: make the inode anonymous so the tmpfs slot is reclaimed

TODO item 2. Three independent defects, one lane:

  A. sys_memfd_create never unlinks its "/tmp/memfd:<name>" node, so every call
     burns one of 128 MAX_TMP_FILES slots forever (shared across /tmp, /dev/shm
     and /run/user). A wl_shm-pool-per-frame client bricks memfd_create
     system-wide with ENOSPC after ~100 frames. MEASURED with the unlink backed
     out: memfd_create returns ENOSPC (-28) at round 113 of 300, and the pool
     exhaustion then takes down four later scmtest subtests as collateral.

  B. tmpfile_owner_of() looks the fd up under the RAW pid while every other
     fd-table entry point canonicalises to the TGID, so a memfd created or
     mmap'd on a non-leader thread resolves to no slot at all. sys_mmap turns
     that into ENOMEM -- the "Failed to create memory pool.: Create(Os { code:
     12, kind: OutOfMemory })" panic of the smithay-clipboard thread in every
     COSMIC session. Separate defect from (A); the errno rules out slot
     exhaustion (that is ENOSPC 28), but (A) cannot land without it because an
     unmarked memfd has no VMO and falls back to the 32 KiB inline path.

  C. export_fd() takes no reference for a TmpFile in flight over SCM_RIGHTS.
     Harmless while the name pins the inode; a use-after-free the moment (A)
     makes the inode nameless, because Mesa's swrast path does
     wl_shm_create_pool(fd) then close(fd) immediately, so the sender's last
     descriptor is gone before the compositor's recvmsg installs it. MEASURED
     with only the export_fd reference backed out: the receiver's mmap of the
     imported fd reads a recycled slot and memfd_inflight_close fails.

scmtest gains two subtests (26/0 -> 28/0): memfd_anonymous_reclaim proves the
slot is really reclaimed (300 rounds > 2 * MAX_TMP_FILES, no manual unlink) and
that ftruncate/mmap still work on a nameless fd; memfd_inflight_close pins the
(C) window open deterministically with a second socketpair -- the child must
close its fork-inherited copy of the memfd first, or the parent's close is not
the last fd-table reference and tmp_release_ephemeral's table scan keeps the
slot alive, which makes the subtest pass even without (C).

